  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + 0.5z²)/2, drawn at 0.4 × loss. It is steeper along x, so plain steps overshoot there when learning rate > 1/3 and diverge when it is above 2/3. The ball starts at x = 1, z = 1.8.",
    "Noise: a Gaussian centred on the true slope, with sd 1.5·√((64−b)/(63·b)) per axis for batch size b. That is the shrink factor for sampling b of 64 points, and it is exactly 0 at 64. A noisy step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "The orange arrow is the next mini-batch step (−rate × noisy slope), and the ball really moves that way. The grey arrow is −rate × true slope. Both are drawn at 2 × the step length.",
    "A run stops after 40 steps (finished), or once loss passes 6, the loss at the bowl's steep edge (diverged). Positions off the drawn bowl are capped at its edge, but the Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed seed. Reset replays the sequence from the start. A new run continues with the next noise values. The ball makes 5 updates per second at 1× speed."
  ]
}